IMPORT prelude
IMPORT daydate
IMPORT `vmu-time`

-- Tests of vmu-time.
-- Each expected value is worked out by hand from the quoted line above it, and written next to the assertion.
-- A period gets a case on the day before, on the day itself and on the day after.

§ `Section 2 -- commencement`

-- src:38 | (2) The remaining provisions of this Act come into operation on 1 July 2004.
-- "The remaining provisions of this Act come into operation on 1 July 2004."
#ASSERT `s 2(2) -- the day on which the remaining provisions come into operation` EQUALS Date 1 7 2004

-- src:37 | (1) This section and sections 1, 3, 9, 11 and 12 come into operation on the day on which this Act receives the Royal Assent.
-- The sections named in subsection (1), and "This section" (section 2), come into operation on the day of the Royal Assent.
#ASSERT `s 2(1) -- the section comes into operation on the day of the Royal Assent` 1
#ASSERT `s 2(1) -- the section comes into operation on the day of the Royal Assent` 2
#ASSERT `s 2(1) -- the section comes into operation on the day of the Royal Assent` 3
#ASSERT `s 2(1) -- the section comes into operation on the day of the Royal Assent` 9
#ASSERT `s 2(1) -- the section comes into operation on the day of the Royal Assent` 11
#ASSERT `s 2(1) -- the section comes into operation on the day of the Royal Assent` 12
-- Every other section is a "remaining provision" (sections 4 to 8 and 10 are in this text; 13 is a section the text does not reach).
#ASSERT NOT `s 2(1) -- the section comes into operation on the day of the Royal Assent` 4
#ASSERT NOT `s 2(1) -- the section comes into operation on the day of the Royal Assent` 5
#ASSERT NOT `s 2(1) -- the section comes into operation on the day of the Royal Assent` 6
#ASSERT NOT `s 2(1) -- the section comes into operation on the day of the Royal Assent` 7
#ASSERT NOT `s 2(1) -- the section comes into operation on the day of the Royal Assent` 8
#ASSERT NOT `s 2(1) -- the section comes into operation on the day of the Royal Assent` 10
#ASSERT NOT `s 2(1) -- the section comes into operation on the day of the Royal Assent` 13

-- The day of the Royal Assent is not stated in sections 1 to 12: the fixture takes the date that the endnote prints.
-- src:144 | The Monetary Units Act 2004 was assented to on 11 May 2004 and came into operation as follows:
#ASSERT `s 2 -- the day on which the section comes into operation` 1 (Date 11 5 2004) EQUALS Date 11 5 2004
#ASSERT `s 2 -- the day on which the section comes into operation` 12 (Date 11 5 2004) EQUALS Date 11 5 2004
#ASSERT `s 2 -- the day on which the section comes into operation` 4 (Date 11 5 2004) EQUALS Date 1 7 2004
#ASSERT `s 2 -- the day on which the section comes into operation` 10 (Date 11 5 2004) EQUALS Date 1 7 2004

-- Section 4 comes into operation on 1 July 2004: the day before it has not, the day itself has, the day after has.
#ASSERT NOT `s 2 -- the section has come into operation by the day` 4 (Date 11 5 2004) (Date 30 6 2004)
#ASSERT     `s 2 -- the section has come into operation by the day` 4 (Date 11 5 2004) (Date 1 7 2004)
#ASSERT     `s 2 -- the section has come into operation by the day` 4 (Date 11 5 2004) (Date 2 7 2004)
-- Section 3 comes into operation on the day of the Royal Assent: 10 May, 11 May, 12 May 2004.
#ASSERT NOT `s 2 -- the section has come into operation by the day` 3 (Date 11 5 2004) (Date 10 5 2004)
#ASSERT     `s 2 -- the section has come into operation by the day` 3 (Date 11 5 2004) (Date 11 5 2004)
#ASSERT     `s 2 -- the section has come into operation by the day` 3 (Date 11 5 2004) (Date 12 5 2004)
-- Section 3 has come into operation before section 4 does: on 30 June 2004 section 3 has, section 4 has not.
#ASSERT     `s 2 -- the section has come into operation by the day` 3 (Date 11 5 2004) (Date 30 6 2004)
#ASSERT NOT `s 2 -- the section has come into operation by the day` 4 (Date 11 5 2004) (Date 30 6 2004)

§ `Sections 5, 6 and 11 -- the financial year`

-- src:67 | Before 1 June in each year the Treasurer must publish in the Government Gazette and in a newspaper circulating generally throughout Victoria notice of—
-- src:68 | (a) the value of the fee unit that is to apply for the financial year commencing on 1 July in that year; and
-- "the financial year commencing on 1 July in that year": in 2025 the year commences on 1 July 2025.
#ASSERT `s 6 -- the day on which the financial year commencing on 1 July in the year commences` 2025 EQUALS Date 1 7 2025
#ASSERT `s 6 -- the day on which the financial year commencing on 1 July in the year commences` 2012 EQUALS Date 1 7 2012

-- src:61 | (6) The amount of a fee unit fixed by the Treasurer that is to apply in respect of a financial year must not exceed the amount that is the product of the value of the fee unit in the previous financial year multiplied by the annual rate for the financial year in which the fee unit is to apply rounded to the nearest cent.
-- "the previous financial year": the one that commenced on 1 July of the year before.
#ASSERT `s 5(6) -- the year in which the previous financial year commences` 2012 EQUALS 2011
#ASSERT `s 5(6) -- the year in which the previous financial year commences` 2013 EQUALS 2012

§ `Section 5(4) -- before 1 March`

-- src:59 | (4) The Treasurer is to fix before 1 March in each year an amount to be known as the annual rate by which the amount of a fee unit or penalty unit is to be adjusted for the next financial year.
-- "before 1 March in each year": the last day is the day before 1 March, which is 29 February in a leap year.
#ASSERT `s 5(4) -- the last day on which the annual rate for the financial year commencing on 1 July in the year may be fixed` 2023 EQUALS Date 28 2 2023
#ASSERT `s 5(4) -- the last day on which the annual rate for the financial year commencing on 1 July in the year may be fixed` 2024 EQUALS Date 29 2 2024
#ASSERT `s 5(4) -- the last day on which the annual rate for the financial year commencing on 1 July in the year may be fixed` 2000 EQUALS Date 29 2 2000
-- 2024: the day before 1 March (29 February), the day itself, the day after.
#ASSERT     `s 5(4) -- the annual rate is fixed before 1 March in the year` 2024 (Date 29 2 2024)
#ASSERT NOT `s 5(4) -- the annual rate is fixed before 1 March in the year` 2024 (Date 1 3 2024)
#ASSERT NOT `s 5(4) -- the annual rate is fixed before 1 March in the year` 2024 (Date 2 3 2024)
-- 2023 (not a leap year): the day before 1 March is 28 February.
#ASSERT     `s 5(4) -- the annual rate is fixed before 1 March in the year` 2023 (Date 28 2 2023)
#ASSERT NOT `s 5(4) -- the annual rate is fixed before 1 March in the year` 2023 (Date 1 3 2023)
#ASSERT NOT `s 5(4) -- the annual rate is fixed before 1 March in the year` 2023 (Date 2 3 2023)
-- The text sets no earlier limit: a day in the August before is also before 1 March.
#ASSERT     `s 5(4) -- the annual rate is fixed before 1 March in the year` 2024 (Date 15 8 2023)

§ `Section 6 -- before 1 June`

-- src:67 | Before 1 June in each year the Treasurer must publish in the Government Gazette and in a newspaper circulating generally throughout Victoria notice of—
-- "Before 1 June in each year": the last day is 31 May.
#ASSERT `s 6 -- the last day on which notice may be published in the year` 2025 EQUALS Date 31 5 2025
#ASSERT `s 6 -- the last day on which notice may be published in the year` 2024 EQUALS Date 31 5 2024
-- Both publications must be before 1 June: the day before (31 May), the day itself, the day after.
#ASSERT     `s 6 -- notice is published in the Gazette and in a newspaper before 1 June in the year` 2025 (Date 31 5 2025) (Date 31 5 2025)
#ASSERT NOT `s 6 -- notice is published in the Gazette and in a newspaper before 1 June in the year` 2025 (Date 1 6 2025) (Date 31 5 2025)
#ASSERT NOT `s 6 -- notice is published in the Gazette and in a newspaper before 1 June in the year` 2025 (Date 31 5 2025) (Date 1 6 2025)
#ASSERT NOT `s 6 -- notice is published in the Gazette and in a newspaper before 1 June in the year` 2025 (Date 2 6 2025) (Date 2 6 2025)
#ASSERT     `s 6 -- notice is published in the Gazette and in a newspaper before 1 June in the year` 2025 (Date 30 5 2025) (Date 28 2 2025)

§ `Section 9(2) -- 1 July 2004`

-- src:96 | (i) to take effect on or before 1 July 2004, the regulation converts fees in monetary amounts to fee units by dividing the amount of the fee by $10; or
-- "to take effect on or before 1 July 2004": 30 June, 1 July and 2 July 2004; and 29 February 2004, a leap day.
#ASSERT `s 9(2) -- 1 July 2004` EQUALS Date 1 7 2004
#ASSERT     `s 9(2) -- the regulation takes effect on or before 1 July 2004` (Date 30 6 2004)
#ASSERT     `s 9(2) -- the regulation takes effect on or before 1 July 2004` (Date 1 7 2004)
#ASSERT NOT `s 9(2) -- the regulation takes effect on or before 1 July 2004` (Date 2 7 2004)
#ASSERT     `s 9(2) -- the regulation takes effect on or before 1 July 2004` (Date 29 2 2004)

-- src:97 | (ii) to take effect after 1 July 2004, the regulation converts fees in monetary amounts to fee units by dividing the amount of the fee by the value of a fee unit fixed by the Treasurer under section 5(3);
-- "to take effect after 1 July 2004": the day before, the day itself and the day after.
#ASSERT NOT `s 9(2) -- the regulation takes effect after 1 July 2004` (Date 30 6 2004)
#ASSERT NOT `s 9(2) -- the regulation takes effect after 1 July 2004` (Date 1 7 2004)
#ASSERT     `s 9(2) -- the regulation takes effect after 1 July 2004` (Date 2 7 2004)

§ `Section 11 -- the financial year commencing on 1 July 2012`

-- src:119 | (1) Despite sections 5 and 6, for the financial year commencing on 1 July 2012—
-- "the financial year commencing on 1 July 2012": the year 2012 is the one, 2011 and 2013 are not.
#ASSERT `s 11 -- the year in which the financial year commencing on 1 July 2012 commences` EQUALS 2012
#ASSERT     `s 11 -- the financial year is the one commencing on 1 July 2012` 2012
#ASSERT NOT `s 11 -- the financial year is the one commencing on 1 July 2012` 2011
#ASSERT NOT `s 11 -- the financial year is the one commencing on 1 July 2012` 2013

-- src:120 | (a) the value of a fee unit is $12.53; and
-- "(a) the value of a fee unit is $12.53".
#ASSERT `s 11(1)(a) -- the value of a fee unit for the financial year commencing on 1 July 2012, in dollars` EQUALS 12.53
-- src:121 | (b) the value of a penalty unit is $140.84.
-- "(b) the value of a penalty unit is $140.84."
#ASSERT `s 11(1)(b) -- the value of a penalty unit for the financial year commencing on 1 July 2012, in dollars` EQUALS 140.84
-- src:123 | (3) Despite section 5, the annual rate for the financial year commencing on 1 July 2012 is taken to be 2⋅5%.
-- "taken to be 2⋅5%": 2.5% is 0.025, and one percent more or less is a different number.
#ASSERT `s 11(3) -- the annual rate for the financial year commencing on 1 July 2012` EQUALS 0.025
#ASSERT `s 11(3) -- the annual rate for the financial year commencing on 1 July 2012` GREATER THAN 0.024
#ASSERT `s 11(3) -- the annual rate for the financial year commencing on 1 July 2012` LESS THAN 0.026

§ `Section 4 and section 7 -- units to dollars`

-- src:53 | If in an Act, a statutory rule or a legislative instrument there is a statement of a number (whether whole, fractional or decimal) of what are called fee units, that statement must, unless the context otherwise requires, be construed as stating a number of dollars equal to the product obtained by multiplying the number of fee units by the amount fixed from time to time by the Treasurer under section 5(3).
-- "a number (whether whole, fractional or decimal) of what are called fee units ... the product obtained by multiplying the number of fee units by the amount fixed".
-- 3 fee units at 12.53 dollars: 3 x 12.53 = 37.59.
#ASSERT `s 4 -- the number of dollars a statement of fee units states` 3 12.53 EQUALS 37.59
-- A fractional number: half a fee unit at 12.53 dollars is 6.265 dollars, not rounded.
#ASSERT `s 4 -- the number of dollars a statement of fee units states` 0.5 12.53 EQUALS 6.265
-- A decimal number: 2.5 fee units at 10 dollars is 25 dollars.
#ASSERT `s 4 -- the number of dollars a statement of fee units states` 2.5 10 EQUALS 25
-- No fee units: no dollars.
#ASSERT `s 4 -- the number of dollars a statement of fee units states` 0 12.53 EQUALS 0

-- src:73 | (1) The amount of a fee provided for by an Act, a statutory rule or a legislative instrument by a fee unit is to be calculated by multiplying the number of fee units applicable by the value of a fee unit fixed under section 5.
-- 3.5 fee units at 12.53 dollars: 3.5 x 12.53 = 43.855 dollars.
#ASSERT `s 7(1) -- the amount of a fee, in dollars` 3.5 12.53 EQUALS 43.855
#ASSERT `s 7(1) -- the amount of a fee, in dollars` 3 12.53 EQUALS 37.59
-- src:74 | (2) The amount of a penalty provided for by an Act or statutory rule by a penalty unit is to be calculated by multiplying the number of penalty units applicable by the value of a penalty unit fixed under section 5.
-- 2 penalty units at 140.84 dollars: 2 x 140.84 = 281.68; one and a half: 211.26; half: 70.42.
#ASSERT `s 7(2) -- the amount of a penalty, in dollars` 2 140.84 EQUALS 281.68
#ASSERT `s 7(2) -- the amount of a penalty, in dollars` 1.5 140.84 EQUALS 211.26
#ASSERT `s 7(2) -- the amount of a penalty, in dollars` 0.5 140.84 EQUALS 70.42

-- src:75 | (3) The amount of a fee calculated under this section may be rounded to the nearest 10 cents.
-- "may be rounded to the nearest 10 cents": 43.855 is nearer 43.90 (0.045 away) than 43.80 (0.055 away).
#ASSERT `s 7(3) -- the amount to which the fee may be rounded, to the nearest 10 cents` 43.855 EQUALS JUST 43.9
-- 12.54 is nearer 12.50, 12.56 is nearer 12.60, 12.5 is already a multiple of 10 cents, 0 stays 0.
#ASSERT `s 7(3) -- the amount to which the fee may be rounded, to the nearest 10 cents` 12.54 EQUALS JUST 12.5
#ASSERT `s 7(3) -- the amount to which the fee may be rounded, to the nearest 10 cents` 12.56 EQUALS JUST 12.6
#ASSERT `s 7(3) -- the amount to which the fee may be rounded, to the nearest 10 cents` 12.5 EQUALS JUST 12.5
#ASSERT `s 7(3) -- the amount to which the fee may be rounded, to the nearest 10 cents` 0 EQUALS JUST 0
-- Just either side of the half: 12.549 and 12.551.
#ASSERT `s 7(3) -- the amount to which the fee may be rounded, to the nearest 10 cents` 12.549 EQUALS JUST 12.5
#ASSERT `s 7(3) -- the amount to which the fee may be rounded, to the nearest 10 cents` 12.551 EQUALS JUST 12.6
-- Exactly half way between 12.50 and 12.60: the text does not say which way a half goes, so the answer is NOTHING.
#ASSERT `s 7(3) -- the amount to which the fee may be rounded, to the nearest 10 cents` 12.55 EQUALS NOTHING
#ASSERT `s 7(3) -- the amount to which the fee may be rounded, to the nearest 10 cents` 12.45 EQUALS NOTHING

-- src:76 | (4) The amount of a penalty calculated under this section may be rounded to the nearest dollar.
-- "may be rounded to the nearest dollar": 281.68 is nearer 282, 211.26 nearer 211, 70.42 nearer 70.
#ASSERT `s 7(4) -- the amount to which the penalty may be rounded, to the nearest dollar` 281.68 EQUALS JUST 282
#ASSERT `s 7(4) -- the amount to which the penalty may be rounded, to the nearest dollar` 211.26 EQUALS JUST 211
#ASSERT `s 7(4) -- the amount to which the penalty may be rounded, to the nearest dollar` 70.42 EQUALS JUST 70
-- A whole number of dollars stays; just either side of the half: 12.49 and 12.51.
#ASSERT `s 7(4) -- the amount to which the penalty may be rounded, to the nearest dollar` 12 EQUALS JUST 12
#ASSERT `s 7(4) -- the amount to which the penalty may be rounded, to the nearest dollar` 12.49 EQUALS JUST 12
#ASSERT `s 7(4) -- the amount to which the penalty may be rounded, to the nearest dollar` 12.51 EQUALS JUST 13
-- Exactly half way: NOTHING.
#ASSERT `s 7(4) -- the amount to which the penalty may be rounded, to the nearest dollar` 12.5 EQUALS NOTHING
#ASSERT `s 7(4) -- the amount to which the penalty may be rounded, to the nearest dollar` 0.5 EQUALS NOTHING

§ `Section 5(6) and (7) -- the product, rounded to the nearest cent`

-- src:61 | (6) The amount of a fee unit fixed by the Treasurer that is to apply in respect of a financial year must not exceed the amount that is the product of the value of the fee unit in the previous financial year multiplied by the annual rate for the financial year in which the fee unit is to apply rounded to the nearest cent.
-- "the product of the value of the fee unit in the previous financial year multiplied by the annual rate ... rounded to the nearest cent", with the annual rate a proportion added to 1.
-- 10 x (1 + 0.025) = 10.25; 12 x (1 + 0.025) = 12.3; 7 x (1 + 0.016) = 7.112, which is 7.11 to the nearest cent.
#ASSERT `s 5(6) -- the product, rounded to the nearest cent` 10 0.025 EQUALS JUST 10.25
#ASSERT `s 5(6) -- the product, rounded to the nearest cent` 12 0.025 EQUALS JUST 12.3
#ASSERT `s 5(6) -- the product, rounded to the nearest cent` 7 0.016 EQUALS JUST 7.11
-- 7 x (1 + 0.017) = 7.119, which is 7.12 to the nearest cent.
#ASSERT `s 5(6) -- the product, rounded to the nearest cent` 7 0.017 EQUALS JUST 7.12
-- 7 x (1 + 0.015) = 7.105 is exactly half way between 7.10 and 7.11: NOTHING.
#ASSERT `s 5(6) -- the product, rounded to the nearest cent` 7 0.015 EQUALS NOTHING
-- The rate is a proportion: 2.5% is 0.025, and 100 x (1 + 0.025) = 102.5.
#ASSERT `s 5(6) -- the product, rounded to the nearest cent` 100 0.025 EQUALS JUST 102.5
#ASSERT `s 5(6) -- the product, rounded to the nearest cent` 0 0.025 EQUALS JUST 0

-- src:62 | (7) The amount of a penalty unit fixed by the Treasurer that is to apply in respect of a financial year must not exceed the amount that is the product of the value of the penalty unit in the previous financial year multiplied by the annual rate for the financial year in which the penalty is to apply rounded to the nearest cent.
-- The same for a penalty unit: 100 x (1 + 0.025) = 102.5; 133 x (1 + 0.0125) = 134.6625, which is 134.66; 4 x (1 + 0.125) = 4.5.
#ASSERT `s 5(7) -- the product, rounded to the nearest cent` 100 0.025 EQUALS JUST 102.5
#ASSERT `s 5(7) -- the product, rounded to the nearest cent` 133 0.0125 EQUALS JUST 134.66
#ASSERT `s 5(7) -- the product, rounded to the nearest cent` 4 0.125 EQUALS JUST 4.5
-- 3 x (1 + 0.0025) = 3.0075 is three quarters of a cent above 3.00, so it is nearer 3.01.
#ASSERT `s 5(7) -- the product, rounded to the nearest cent` 3 0.0025 EQUALS JUST 3.01
-- 1.005 is exactly half way between 1.00 and 1.01: NOTHING.
#ASSERT `s 5(7) -- the product, rounded to the nearest cent` 1 0.005 EQUALS NOTHING

§ `Section 9 -- conversion into units, and the roundings it deems`

-- src:96 | (i) to take effect on or before 1 July 2004, the regulation converts fees in monetary amounts to fee units by dividing the amount of the fee by $10; or
-- "(i) ... dividing the amount of the fee by $10": 25 dollars is 2.5 fee units; 25.5 dollars is 2.55; 0 is 0.
#ASSERT `s 9(2)(a)(i) -- the number of fee units when the fee is divided by 10 dollars` 25 EQUALS 2.5
#ASSERT `s 9(2)(a)(i) -- the number of fee units when the fee is divided by 10 dollars` 25.5 EQUALS 2.55
#ASSERT `s 9(2)(a)(i) -- the number of fee units when the fee is divided by 10 dollars` 0 EQUALS 0
-- src:97 | (ii) to take effect after 1 July 2004, the regulation converts fees in monetary amounts to fee units by dividing the amount of the fee by the value of a fee unit fixed by the Treasurer under section 5(3);
-- "(ii) ... dividing the amount of the fee by the value of a fee unit": 25 by 12.5 is 2; 30 by 12 is 2.5; a value of 0 has no answer.
#ASSERT `s 9(2)(a)(ii) -- the number of fee units when the fee is divided by the value of a fee unit` 25 12.5 EQUALS JUST 2
#ASSERT `s 9(2)(a)(ii) -- the number of fee units when the fee is divided by the value of a fee unit` 30 12 EQUALS JUST 2.5
#ASSERT `s 9(2)(a)(ii) -- the number of fee units when the fee is divided by the value of a fee unit` 25 0 EQUALS NOTHING
-- src:99 | (i) to take effect on or before 1 July 2004, the regulation converts penalties in monetary amounts to penalty units by dividing the amount of the penalty by $100; or
-- "(i) ... dividing the amount of the penalty by $100": 250 dollars is 2.5 penalty units; 99 dollars is 0.99.
#ASSERT `s 9(2)(b)(i) -- the number of penalty units when the penalty is divided by 100 dollars` 250 EQUALS 2.5
#ASSERT `s 9(2)(b)(i) -- the number of penalty units when the penalty is divided by 100 dollars` 99 EQUALS 0.99
-- src:100 | (ii) to take effect after 1 July 2004, the regulation converts penalties in monetary amounts to penalty units by dividing the amount of the penalty by the value of a penalty unit fixed by the Treasurer under section 5(3);
-- "(ii) ... dividing the amount of the penalty by the value of a penalty unit": 281.68 by 140.84 is 2; a value of 0 has no answer.
#ASSERT `s 9(2)(b)(ii) -- the number of penalty units when the penalty is divided by the value of a penalty unit` 281.68 140.84 EQUALS JUST 2
#ASSERT `s 9(2)(b)(ii) -- the number of penalty units when the penalty is divided by the value of a penalty unit` 100 0 EQUALS NOTHING

-- src:107 | (3) For the purposes of subsection (2)(a) and (b), in calculating the number of fee units or penalty units, the number of fee units or penalty units is deemed to be calculated in accordance with those provisions if the fee unit or penalty unit is rounded up to the next 2 decimal places.
-- "rounded up to the next 2 decimal places": 3.456 up to 3.46; 3.454 also up to 3.46; a number with 2 decimal places stays.
#ASSERT `s 9(3) -- the number rounded up to the next 2 decimal places` 3.456 EQUALS 3.46
#ASSERT `s 9(3) -- the number rounded up to the next 2 decimal places` 3.454 EQUALS 3.46
#ASSERT `s 9(3) -- the number rounded up to the next 2 decimal places` 3.46 EQUALS 3.46
#ASSERT `s 9(3) -- the number rounded up to the next 2 decimal places` 3.4601 EQUALS 3.47
#ASSERT `s 9(3) -- the number rounded up to the next 2 decimal places` 2 EQUALS 2

-- src:108 | (4) For the purposes of subsection (2)(f), in calculating the amount of a fee, the amount of the fee is deemed to be calculated in accordance with that provision if the fee is rounded to the nearest 10 cents.
-- "rounded to the nearest 10 cents": 43.855 to 43.9; 43.85 is half way between 43.80 and 43.90: NOTHING.
#ASSERT `s 9(4) -- the amount of the fee rounded to the nearest 10 cents` 43.855 EQUALS JUST 43.9
#ASSERT `s 9(4) -- the amount of the fee rounded to the nearest 10 cents` 43.84 EQUALS JUST 43.8
#ASSERT `s 9(4) -- the amount of the fee rounded to the nearest 10 cents` 43.85 EQUALS NOTHING
-- src:109 | (5) For the purposes of subsection (2)(g), in calculating the amount of a penalty, the amount of the penalty is deemed to be calculated in accordance with that provision if the penalty is rounded to the nearest dollar.
-- "rounded to the nearest dollar": 281.68 to 282; 281.4 to 281; 281.5 is half way: NOTHING.
#ASSERT `s 9(5) -- the amount of the penalty rounded to the nearest dollar` 281.68 EQUALS JUST 282
#ASSERT `s 9(5) -- the amount of the penalty rounded to the nearest dollar` 281.4 EQUALS JUST 281
#ASSERT `s 9(5) -- the amount of the penalty rounded to the nearest dollar` 281.5 EQUALS NOTHING

§ `Section 9(1)(g) and (2)(h) -- the cumulative annual rates`

-- src:93 | (g) increase any fees specified in monetary amounts in the statutory rule by the amount of the cumulative annual rates fixed by the Treasurer under this Act for the period that the fee has had effect without any increase.
-- "the cumulative annual rates ... for the period that the fee has had effect without any increase".
-- No year: 0. One year at 0.025: 0.025. Two years at 0.025: 1.025 x 1.025 - 1 = 0.050625. Three years at 0.1: 1.331 - 1 = 0.331.
#ASSERT `s 9(1)(g) -- the cumulative annual rate` EMPTY EQUALS 0
#ASSERT `s 9(1)(g) -- the cumulative annual rate` (LIST 0.025) EQUALS 0.025
#ASSERT `s 9(1)(g) -- the cumulative annual rate` (LIST 0.025, 0.025) EQUALS 0.050625
#ASSERT `s 9(1)(g) -- the cumulative annual rate` (LIST 0.1, 0.1, 0.1) EQUALS 0.331
-- A fee of 5 dollars with two years at 0.025: 5 x 0.050625 = 0.253125.
#ASSERT `s 9(1)(g) -- the amount of the increase` 5 (LIST 0.025, 0.025) EQUALS 0.253125
#ASSERT `s 9(1)(g) -- the amount of the increase` 5 EMPTY EQUALS 0

-- src:106 | (h) in the case of a regulation made under subsection (1)(g), the regulation increases any fees that are less than the value of one fee unit by an amount not exceeding the cumulative annual rates fixed by the Treasurer under this Act for the period that the fee has had effect without any increase.
-- "an amount not exceeding the cumulative annual rates": 0.25 is under 0.253125, 0.253125 is on it, 0.26 is over.
#ASSERT     `s 9(2)(h) -- the increase does not exceed the cumulative annual rates` 5 (LIST 0.025, 0.025) 0.25
#ASSERT     `s 9(2)(h) -- the increase does not exceed the cumulative annual rates` 5 (LIST 0.025, 0.025) 0.253125
#ASSERT NOT `s 9(2)(h) -- the increase does not exceed the cumulative annual rates` 5 (LIST 0.025, 0.025) 0.26
